Here is a C++ class using SFML to represent a spritesheet and index specific sprites:
```cpp
#include <SFML/Graphics.hpp>

class SpriteSheet {
public:
    SpriteSheet(const std::string& filename, int spriteWidth, int spriteHeight) {
        texture.loadFromFile(filename);
        spriteSize = sf::Vector2i(spriteWidth, spriteHeight);
        spriteCount = texture.getSize().x / spriteWidth;
    }

    sf::Sprite getSprite(int index) {
        sf::Sprite sprite;
        sprite.setTexture(texture);
        sprite.setTextureRect(sf::IntRect(index * spriteSize.x, 0, spriteSize.x, spriteSize.y));
        return sprite;
    }

private:
    sf::Texture texture;
    sf::Vector2i spriteSize;
    int spriteCount;
};
```

This class loads a spritesheet texture from a file. It keeps track of the width and height of each sprite, as well as the total sprite count.
The getSprite() method can then return a SFML Sprite object for a specific sprite index, with the correct texture rect set for that sprite.

You can use it like this:
```cpp
SpriteSheet spritesheet("spritesheet.png", 64, 64);

sf::Sprite sprite1 = spritesheet.getSprite(0);
sf::Sprite sprite2 = spritesheet.getSprite(1);
// etc...
```

This will give you SFML Sprite objects for each sprite in the spritesheet. Let me know if you have any other questions!

Here are the steps to configure TIM0 on an Atmega 328p:

1. Select the timer mode. The 328p supports normal, PWM phase correct, CTC, and fast PWM modes. For this example, we'll use normal mode.

2. Set the prescaler. The prescaler determines how fast the timer counts. We'll set a prescaler of 64, so the timer increments every 64 clock cycles.

3. Set the timer overflow value. In normal mode, the timer counts up to this value, then overflows and resets to 0. We'll set an overflow value of 999.

4. Enable the overflow interrupt. This will trigger an interrupt whenever the timer overflows. We'll enable the TOIE0 interrupt.

5. Set the timer to count up. In normal mode, set the WGM00 and WGM01 bits to 0. 

6. Enable the timer. Set the CS00, CS01, and CS02 bits to 011 to select the prescaler of 64. Then set the TOV0 flag to enable the timer.

7. Write the ISR. We'll write the TIMER0_OVF_vect ISR to run whenever the overflow interrupt triggers.

8. Put it all together. Here is the full code:

```c
void setup() {
  // Set timer mode to normal
  TCCR0A = 0;  

  // Set prescaler to 64 and enable timer 
  TCCR0B = 0b00000111;  

  // Set overflow value to 999
  OCR0A = 999;  

  // Enable overflow interrupt
  TIMSK0 = 1;  

  // Set timer to count up
  TCCR0A &= ~(1 << WGM00);
  TCCR0A &= ~(1 << WGM01);
}

ISR(TIMER0_OVF_vect) {
  // Timer overflow interrupt code  
}

void loop() {
  // Main code
} 
```

This will configure TIM0 to overflow and trigger an interrupt every 64 * 999 = 63,936 clock cycles. Let me know if you have any other questions!

