Sí, es posible convertir código JavaScript a TypeScript. Aquí hay algunos pasos para hacer la conversión:

1. Cambiar las extensiones de archivo de .js a .ts. Esto le indicará a TypeScript que compile el archivo.

2. Agregar tipos de datos. Declare el tipo de variables, parámetros de función y valores de retorno de función. Por ejemplo:

```js
let x = 5;         // JavaScript

let x: number = 5; // TypeScript
```

3. Convertir variables var a let o const. TypeScript prefiere el alcance de bloque con let y const.

4. Convertir funciones a arrow functions o agregar tipos a los parámetros y valores de retorno. Por ejemplo: 

```js
function add(x, y) {
  return x + y; 
}

let add = (x: number, y: number): number => x + y; // TypeScript
```

5. Importar y exportar módulos. TypeScript tiene un sistema de módulos más robusto. Por ejemplo:

```js
const x = require('./x');
module.exports = x;

// TypeScript 
import x from './x';
export default x; 
```

6. Agregar interfaces para describir la forma de los objetos. Por ejemplo:

```js
let user = {
  name: "John",
  age: 30 
};

interface User {
  name: string;
  age: number; 
}

let user: User = {
  name: "John",
  age: 30 
};
```

7. Agregar tipos a literales de objetos. Por ejemplo:

```js
let obj = {
  x: 5,
  y: 10
};

let obj = {
  x: 5,
  y: 10 
} as const; // TypeScript
```

Eso cubre las bases para convertir JavaScript a TypeScript. Por supuesto, hay más características en TypeScript que puedes aprovechar, pero eso te dará una buena base para comenzar.

Here is a basic proof of concept for a horizontally scalable, probabilistic database in Rust using Raft:

```rust
use raft::{Client, Config, Error, RawNode, State};

struct Database {
    raft: RawNode<StateMachine>,
}

impl Database {
    fn new(config: Config) -> Result<Database, Error> {
        let raft = RawNode::new(config, StateMachine)?;
        Ok(Database { raft })
    }

    fn get(&self, key: String) -> Option<String> {
        self.raft.apply_command(GetCommand { key }, false).unwrap()
    }

    fn set(&self, key: String, value: String) -> Result<(), Error> {
        self.raft.apply_command(SetCommand { key, value }, true)
    } 
}

struct StateMachine {
    kv_store: HashMap<String, String>,
}

impl raft::StateMachine for StateMachine {
    type Command = Command;
    type Query = Query;
    type Response = Response;

    fn apply_command(&mut self, cmd: Command) {
        match cmd {
            SetCommand { key, value } => {
                self.kv_store.insert(key, value);
            }
            GetCommand { key } => {}
        }
    }

    fn query(&self, query: Query) -> Option<Response> {
        match query {
            GetQuery { key } => self.kv_store.get(&key).map(Response::Value),
        }
    }
}

enum Command {
    SetCommand { key: String, value: String },
    GetCommand { key: String }, 
}

enum Query {
    GetQuery { key: String } 
}

enum Response {
    Value(String),
}
```

This defines a `Database` struct which wraps a `RawNode` from the `raft` crate. The `StateMachine` implements the `raft::StateMachine` trait to handle commands and queries. Commands like `SetCommand` are replicated using Raft consensus, while reads are served locally.

This is a very basic POC, but it shows the overall structure of building a probabilistic database on top of Raft in Rust. Let me know if you have any other questions!

To add your own content to the ESP32 WiFiManager portal, you can do the following:

1. Create an HTML page with your content. For example:

```html
<h1>My Custom Page</h1>
<p>This is some custom content I want to show in the portal</p>
```

2. Convert the HTML into a string in your Arduino code:

```c++
String customPage = "<h1>My Custom Page</h1><p>This is some custom content I want to show in the portal</p>";
```

3. Add the page to the WiFiManager using:

```c++
WiFiManager wifiManager;

// Add custom page
wifiManager.addPage("custom", customPage); 
```

4. In your WiFiManager portal, there will now be a new "Custom" page option that shows your content.

5. You can also set the custom page as the landing page that is shown first when the portal is opened:

```c++ 
wifiManager.setCustomPage("custom");
```

6. If you want to add a custom page with input elements like text inputs, you need to also register a handler to save the input data:

```c++
wifiManager.addPage("form", customPageWithForm);
wifiManager.setSaveConfigCallback(saveConfigCallback);

// Save config callback 
void saveConfigCallback() {
  String ssid = server->arg("ssid");
  String pass = server->arg("pass");
  // Save SSID and password to EEPROM, etc...
}
```

This will call your callback when the "Save" button is pressed on your custom page.

Hope this helps you add custom content to your ESP32 WiFiManager! Let me know if you have any other questions.

